## U-KAN SEGMENTATION MODEL -> Fine tuning 
GOAL -> Predict terrain class for EVERY pixel
256×256×5 tile -> 256×256 segmentation mask

Each pixel gets: vegetation probability , snow probability , barren land probability , etc.

encoder -> compresses detail like edges , textures , terrain structures , spectral patterns
## sementic meaning 
KAN eneventually learns: 
High NIR + texture pattern
= vegetation(this region is likely alpine vegetation)

High visible reflectance + cold terrain texture
= snow/glacier (this region resembles glacier edge)
ETC...


WHY DO WE NEED KAN ?
The relationship of terrain is not simple its non-linear , unlike CAN which detect linear data KAN is used to 
detect non-linear data. In nature, the terrain are non linear KAN models these nonlinear interactions better.

## U-KAN = 
U-Net = spatial understanding(Shapes , boundry,  segmentation)
KAN = complex nonlinear relationships(spectral interactions , terrain physics , environmental dependencies)

